y using the vaccine the miracle  of which is available to us  is something that needs the coordination  of the leaders only they can release the  vaccines not the imf or the world health  organization the leaders have got to  make that decision they ordered the  vaccines they over ordered and they can  now make them available  if i recall correctly when we discussed  it before actually on the  very day  i think that the president of the united  states was calling a meeting  of uh his fellow g7 leaders to do  exactly what you've just said  why didn't that  happen i mean why didn't we hear more  about that  well i think it was a tragedy that the  meeting  went round the table i don't think boris  johnson was there from the information  that i have although he was down to  speak as right at the beginning of the  event it went round the table people  made promises but they didn't add up to  being enough to get the vaccinations to  people at the time that they needed them  and so we have promises about next year  and we have some vaccines going this  year but we'll never meet the 40 target  unless we take action now and that's an  airlift it needs an airlift now  boris did you say that boris johnson  wasn't in the room when they were  talking about this  well downing street has refused to  confirm that he was there  and yet he made the promise that he was  going to get the world vaccinated and  the big summit that was to be held and  was held under the office of president  biden if as people are saying he did not  attend and i think he's better answer to  the house of commons about why a promise  was made at the g7 that he was going to  oversee and then when it came to the  meetings that had to deal with at a  vaccine summit including the secretary  general of the united nations president  of the european union uh the prime  minister of canada everybody else was  there prime minister draghi of italy  we have to ask what role did he play in  that summit  can i just uh briefly return to the uh  issue of this morning  and ask you about the tone of politics  right now  i spoke both to the home secretary and  to lisa nandy about this and both of  them  i think seem to  uh  confirm what a lot of people are saying  that  without wanting to trace this particular  event to  uh you know the wider political  discourse that there is some  an edge an unpleasantness a coarseness  that's entered  uh political speech and debate  which uh  from their point of view we could well  do without what is your view  yeah i think you can disagree without  being disagreeable and i think  you can have discord without having  malice  and too often i think political  arguments descend  into personal  attacks and personal  uh conflicts  and i think when it comes to the social  media we've got to look again at the way  these algorithms are organized  because they push people towards the  most extreme positions  and and the media looks to me like a  shouting match without an umpire but the  most extreme positions are the ones that  come across  now this is i think partly because of  the way that um the different media  organizations organize their information  and i think something's got to be done  to look at  online harm that is resulting from this  so yes we've got to clean up here but  let us also remember that if this is a  terrorist incident we are dealing with a  group of terrorists or a group  around a terrorist  who believe that coexistence with the  west is impossible  and we have got to show by the strength  of our democracy and the strength by  which we reach out to other countries  and call for cooperation that this is  not the way that we see the world and  this is not the way the world has got to  be  terrorism has got to be fought directly  head on  gordon brown thank you very much indeed  thank you  tributes to sir david amos have come in  from across the political divide with  many pointing to his commitment to the  local area and his constituents i want  to speak now to two members of  parliament from the essex region the  conservative mp for romford andrew  rossendell who's been friends with david  for 40 years and the labour mp for  ilford north west streeting um  thank you gentlemen for joining us it's  unusual for us to have  two members of parliament on together  especially from different  parties but i'd like you both to reflect  on your  experience not as partisans but as  constituency mps who who  were  close to david um  and i just would like starting with you  andrew uh  if you want to say something about your  memories of him  david was a wonderful person he was a  really genuine friend  we've all got friends in parliament he  was special because he really cared  about  the people around him he cared about his  constituency  he was a passionate person and he had  strong beliefs he he was uncompromising  what he stood for  but the things he stood for  were wonderful causes for instance  animal welfare he was  he was a huge animal welfare supporter  and  the other thing i'd say about him is  that  he would  always have time for everybody he met  you know he didn't just brush you off he  he would spend time talking to all all  the people he met  and nothing would be too much he never  looked down on anyone he was he he was  an equal to everyone else that's the way  he  he behaved and  we're going to miss him he was a  fantastic mp and a great friend and  for him to lose him in this such tragic  circumstances  is very difficult for us to accept and  understand it really is it's a very sad  moment for all of us  we're streaming  yeah i think we were just discussing  before we came on um david was one of  those great characters in parliament  everyone knew him because you could not  know him he was  renowned for as andrew said the cause he  took up  a terrific sense of humor and also  just the most outstanding champion for  his constituency and i've not checked  but i would put money on the fact that  no one has ever mentioned south end in  parliament more than sir david did and  i'm really glad actually that the  campaign for city status for south end  is um you know really up at the top of  the agenda because i could think of no  more fitting tribute to him and he was  loved right across the political  spectrum and you've heard that in recent  days from some of my labor colleagues  who absolutely love david loved working  for him and i don't think that  there's any real comfort that we could  offer his family today but i hope they  draw some small comfort from just how  much  you know a husband a father was just  loved right across the political  spectrum in the house of commons and  evidently loved by his constituents as  we've seen in the outpouring of grief  and love for david in the last day or so  the conversation this morning has  centered very much on the security  of members of parliament and  i would guess also local councillors who  have to meet people they don't know face  to face um and there's some suggestion  that actually over recent times  things have become  more dangerous uh certainly rougher  has either of you  have you had to take uh extra  precautions over your period uh as  members of parliament andrew rossendale  first  um yes i'm afraid i've been mp 20 years  and during that period  uh i see  a lot of more nasty people around than  ever before and they're willing to say  and do things  in a way that i would never thought  possible in this country and we all have  to be really aware and keep our wits  about us the abuse we get on social  media by emails i mean we get it all the  time but we brush it off because we've  got a job to do and  we tell the police the police have got  other things to do it's not always  followed up  and i think probably this is all going  to change we've got to take this a bit  more seriously in future i've had my  office was arsoned my car was smashed up  someone tried to get into my house and  injure me managed to stop them all these  things have happened over the years but  you take it in your stride because  our priority is to to get on with our  job and we we we do tend to not think  much about our own security i'm afraid  but i'm thinking now maybe we should do  a bit more um  pretty patel's reviewing  how we're going to be how this is going  to be dealt with in future certainly our  constituency surgeries there's going to  be have to be some new arrangements for  those but we can't let evil destroy  democracy and this was pure evil what  happened here we cannot allow that to  undermine the democratic traditions of  this country so whatever happens  where's myself and all members of  parliament we're going to be out there  serving our constituents we're not going  to let this defeat us we'll still be  there and doing the job we're elected to  do  we're streeting andrew rosendale says we  have to do a bit more  what in your view would a bit more in  practical terms constitute  uh i think it's a good question trevor  and i don't know what the answer is to  be honest um  i think  the thing about the accessibility of  politicians in britain and it's when  i've met politicians from other  countries they they remark upon it it's  not just that we do it because we feel  obliged to and we think it's part of the  job we do it because we love doing it we  love spending time in our communities we  love spending time with our constituents  and we you know we're people people we  like spending time with people that's  why we're in politics to make a  difference and to serve people so so i  think  none of us really want to change that we  don't want to take measures that that  undermine that but we're having  difficult conversations with our own  families this is not just about us it's  about the safety of our staff the people  who work for us it's about you know how  our families feel when we go off to work  and the conversation that particularly  you know our colleagues who are parents  have been having with their children in  the last day or so about why our job's  important and why it's worth the risk  but you know this is the second  second  incident like this in five years and you  know we've been stricken with grief for  david grief for joe  and you know and also grief for the job  that we do in a sense because  you know  we love it we love what we do david was  the epitome of loving public service  um so we know that something's got to  change but it you know i think  it's so raw and we don't want to kind of  rush to kind of  snap judgments but we know something's  got to change  there's a lot of talk about reviews and  consideration and so on but  are we going to be here again in  three years four years five years  uh as frankly we were on to  the joe cox murder where everybody said  we have things have to change  but actually  it's hard to  identify a practical  measure that has been taken  where's  i i i really hope not um  you know the truth is that we  we are exposed every day to risk but  i want to take it serious but i also  think that there are lots of people who  are exposed to risk every day who serve  the public  we're not unique in that sense and we're  not special case um  and i don't want people watching your  program this morning who would be  outstanding public servants whether as  counselors or members of parliament or  working in other public facing roles to  think this is no longer for me i don't  want to do that i i can't tell you  trevor how much i love doing this job um  and i know i speak for andrew on this  because you know  lived in your constituency at one point  parliamentary neighbors even though  andrew drives around my constituency  every election with his loudhailer  trying to get me out you know i can sit  around you a cross-party friend um we  love doing this job absolutely love it  and i wouldn't want anyone to be put off  but part of that has got to be i'm sure  making sure we're safe  andrew rosendale um  many people would say that  part of what we are seeing now  is an atmosphere that is generated by  a heightened  rhetoric  and the way that people talk about talk  about and to each other in politics at  not least members of parliament  themselves  to what extent  do you think that this changed  atmosphere actually  is the responsibility of  parliamentarians themselves call you  know people who call others enemies of  the people people who call other people  scum for example  so  we're all in politics  and we accept that we are going to be  criticized for our views not everyone  agrees with our opinions and i don't  mind a political debate i don't mind  people attacking me because they  disagree with my views on whatever  subject it may be however there is a  level to which that's it is acceptable  to have that kind of debate if you use  nasty hateful language if you if you use  language which is going to  place you in a position whereby you look  like you  you hate that person because their views  that's not acceptable and there are  some politicians who have said things  they shouldn't have said  um i don't think that's been the cause  of this though it may be part of the  background to i don't know we'll find  out in due course but we we all have a  responsibility to show respect for each  other to show kindness um despite our  political differences and wesley are  probably example of that you know we're  neighboring seats we've never had a bad  word to say about each other that quite  the opposite  but we have different views on issues um  we always will because we're different  parties but it doesn't stop us being  friends and colleagues it doesn't stop  us working together on issues and we  agree on far more than the public think  actually  it's amazing how many mps on other side  of the house that i agree with on issues  and it's not all it's not all  bad feeling between us and sometimes the  media can project it like we're all at  each other but we're not  there's a lot of things that we agree on  and work together on  uh but we've got to be careful of our  language and we've all got to make sure  that when we speak in public and when we  tweet things out and when we issue  statements that that we show respect for  other people even if they're  diametrically opposed to our particular  point of view  we're streeting um  i  remember  after uh  joe cox died  much of the language that we're hearing  this weekend of kindness and  more in common than we that separates us  and so on being used and people  promising that they would be more  respectful and so on um  i think it took roughly six weeks for  members of parliament to be yelling at  each other across the  aisle again  now  you know you two  are not known particularly for that kind  of thing  what can we actually do  to make sure that all of the good  intentions that are going to be  expressed today and tomorrow and the day  after  last into next year  well it takes it takes work and it takes  some effort and  you know i think you're a bit too  generous there i can i think if you ask  the speaker or successive speakers i've  got quite a loud voice in the commons  chamber and the irony is as much as  people say they don't like the the the  jeering and the yelling at prime  minister's questions it's the most  watched part of the week um  i i think that  the the the the rough and tumble of  politics the you know the the rgb of  debate that's part of it i think there  are times when you know intemperate  language can go too far and i wish that  it wouldn't take awful tragedies like  this for us to  remind ourselves and and particularly to  remind the public that we do actually  quite like each other actually across  the political spectrum there are lots of  cross-party friendships and as i say  whenever i do a school visit in my  constituency when i look across the  commons chamber to conservative mps i  don't see a bunch of people who are evil  i see people who 