as just beaming with  pride  because  the dream of having  a huge capacity audience watching  musicians with learning disabilities  inspire  that audience that iconic venue  had come true and largely that was  thanks to the help from sir david  can you tell us a bit about  how he worked with you his involvement  in the charity  yes well i mean i've known sir david for  over 25 years initially just as a  musician in in his constituency  and then after a while he he lent his  support to my work and he would always  ask me  david what can i do to help what can i  do to help you fulfill your dreams  how can i help your charity  all the time that's what he would say  and  for me that was to  open doors you see  i'm a musician and a teacher  but sir david amos  had the access the the influence  to campaign for my charities for greater  opportunities and you must remember that  only a few decades ago people with  learning disabilities would have been  hidden away  there's no  real chance for them to change their  destiny and to create opportunities like  that and  we went on this journey together and he  opened doors that i couldn't open he  he campaigned he fundraised he he just  told everybody and he we had this  wonderful  way of coming up with grand ideas and  grand plans uh the london palladium  breaking a world record performing to  members of the royal family and  ultimately the albert hall and  you know we were then going to go on and  try and get a concert tour to broadway  he was such an exciting  man to know because i always felt that  everything was possible with sir david  and  he was just so proud of south end  you are in leoncy which is uh just a  little bit outside of south and um in  fact i lived in leoncy for a little  while when i worked at an  oil refinery along the estuary it's a  very quiet place it's a dormitory town  um it's possibly the last place you  would have  imagined something like this happening  what impact do you think it's going to  have 